Description: OpenZFS built into the kernel
 The kernel links OpenZFS in, as it does HFS. Built in, ZFS uses the
 kernel's vnode_iocount and kauth_wellknown_guid and has no kmod entry
 points. A read-only dataset passes the root check pivot_root makes, so
 process 1 can switch to it. The SPL takes the machine headers, for arm64,
 runs in 256 MiB, and sizes its first heap segment from the memory, so a
 1 GB board keeps most of its memory. Only zfs_boot= names the pool to
 import for the root: rd= and rootdev= start no pool search, and a pool
 without bootfs says so. Creating a dataset's proxy device needs the same
 policy as removing it, and a dataset gets at most one, with one created
 at a time. A write, a write through a mapping or a truncation by a user
 without the privilege clears the set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits of an
 executable file. The root mount names its dataset as the mount's source,
 so the root dataset shows as mounted. The PMULL check reads the AES
 field of the instruction set register, where PMULL is reported. The
 fork's GHASH fix, patch 0005a, makes the ARMv8 GHASH give the generic
 GHASH's tags. It is the fastest where the CPU has PMULL. The fastest
 choice tests HAVE_AESV8, as the rest of gcm.c does, not HAVE_ARMV8,
 which nothing defines. A file's attributes include its type, so a file
 vdev opens as a regular file, and file vdevs are closed on unmount only
 while the system shuts down, not under I/O. A zvol's I/O from IOKit runs
 on the zvol taskqs, completes asynchronously and reports its errors, so
 a pool on a zvol does not hold the disk threads the zvol's own pool
 needs. The last close refuses new I/O and waits for the I/O queued, and
 a cache flush waits for the I/O queued before it. The zvol device node's
 open and close no longer take the zvol's state lock twice. A remount
 does not make a dataset writable while its readonly property is on, or
 when the property cannot be read. A remount must name the dataset that
 is mounted, and keeps the mount's source: one naming another dataset
 renamed the mount. A remount holds the teardown lock as a reader, so a
 rollback or receive cannot swap the objset under it. The snapdir
 callback purges the name cache only when the value changes, and not
 while a rollback or receive holds the teardown lock as a writer, where a
 vnode reclaim it starts could deadlock. A
 last zvol close that waited for the suspend lock keeps it, and a retry
 does not count a suspend lock it gave up. The root pool's search probes
 only leaf media, and a whole disk only once its matching has settled,
 so the old labels at the end of a card written again are not taken for
 the pool.
 A whole disk that has not settled is probed again later and does not
 hold up the import, and each pass frees its list of pool configs.
 Looking up a mounted dataset by name no longer waits for the mount while
 it holds the pool: it tries with vfs_busy_try() and, if a remount holds
 the mount, lets go of everything and tries again. A temporary property
 is read without making the mount busy.
 While the property callbacks are registered, they keep the mount's own
 options in the flags they set.
